      {
        "id": "source-7fd75596-2b4b-4bcd-8799-d7fc479f343a",
        "projectId": "project-8bb7aecc-fc96-4531-ab19-fb3b29875f93",
        "title": "Example notes · Planning a research brief",
        "kind": "example",
        "text": "Begin with an output you can inspect. “Research the topic” leaves the finish line unclear. “Write a one-page brief with a claim, evidence, and an unresolved question” gives the session a concrete purpose.\n\nSeparate evidence from interpretation. Keep the passage that informed your idea, then write your own explanation beside it. A link back to the passage makes a later disagreement easier to investigate.\n\nDependencies change the order of work. A draft may need selected evidence first; a review needs an existing draft. A plan that ignores those relationships can look full while putting work in an impossible order.\n\nTime is a constraint, not a promise. Estimates and short breaks must fit the available window. When they do not, reduce the scope or move work to another day. End a session by recording the next specific action.",
        "paragraphs": [
          {
            "id": "p-0-ryj2ye",
            "text": "Begin with an output you can inspect. “Research the topic” leaves the finish line unclear. “Write a one-page brief with a claim, evidence, and an unresolved question” gives the session a concrete purpose.",
